.TH "LE" "1" "Jul 2026" "le" ""

.SH NAME
le-stop - Stop a listener (by port/pid) or every listener under a directory


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBle stop [port|pid] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Stop a listener on a port (or the process with a PID), each via its
recommended strategy (TERM / brew services / docker). The PID is
re-checked first, so a recycled PID is never the one that gets signalled.

.PP
With --dir, stop every listener whose working directory is that path or
nested under it — the terminal equivalent of the app's folder-stop, for
clearing out everything a project spun up.

.PP
Use --dry-run to see exactly what would be stopped without touching
anything — worth doing before a --dir sweep.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-d\fP, \fB--dir\fP=""
	stop every listener whose working directory is under this path

.PP
\fB-n\fP, \fB--dry-run\fP[=false]
	print what would be stopped without stopping it

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for stop


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBle(1)\fP


